A||N||14||bHooper||help with, stubborn and broken crossover bolts||||1||
Z||000000||bHooper||01-27-2001||10:38 PM||||i have some problems with the crossover to manifold bolts, on the left side.  <P>the upper one is broken, where there is only threads in the nut(which is attached to the flange and attached to the manifold).  the lower one, is not broken, but it appears that  it is frozen and the head is stripping some.<P>any good suggestions for removing these bolts, without removing the manifold?<P>thanks!<BR>hoop<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||01-28-2001||01:19 AM||||Go back in time, and apply antiseize compound to the bolts before they were installed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>If your time machine is out of order, the only thing I can suggest is lots of Liquid Wrench (or equivalent), then vise grips on the messed up bolt head, if there's enough to grab.  After that, all I know to do is  drill the bolts out and retap.  And, if misery loves company, you can take solace in the fact that I am experiencing essentially the same situation with my GT.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  ||||reg||12
Z||000002||bHooper||01-28-2001||08:57 AM||||Stu... I feel so much better knowing that someone else on the forum is currently experiencing the same form of hell that I am!<P>Thanks for the advice.  I think if I could get the ice grips in there, I could get the blow torch in.  With that, everything is always fine!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||01-28-2001||10:51 AM||||oh well, so much for drilling.<P>i tried, with several bits, but that bolt is extreemly stubborn!  i couldn't get it to drill very well.<P>one of these days i need to get rid of my $29 chuckless black & decker drill... i think that thing is keeping me from finishing too many jobs.<P>tomorrow, i will get a grind stone and grind the head of the bolt off, so that i can get the crossover out.  then i should ::fingers crossed:: be able to grind it down to the manifold flange, so that i can drill that out.<P>unless anyone has a better idea?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||01-28-2001||11:01 AM||||I had this happen to me. I had a buddy with oxy/ACETYLENE torches I heated the area around the broken bolt until it glowed and grabbed on what was left of the stud with vice grips. It turned like butter once it was good and hot.||||reg||4
Z||000005||StuGood||01-28-2001||09:08 PM||||This probably will be about as helpful as the time machine idea (above), but as long as you're talking about using a torch...<P>I heard of using beeswax to get seized set screws out of an old (Auburn or Cord or something) engine block.  It was a hex recess screw, and it wasn't about to come out, after being in there for eighty years.  This guy heated the screw cherry red with a torch, then touched the screw with a block of beeswax.  The wax kind of "wicked" into the threads, and then he just spun it out with 2 fingers and an Allen wrench.  I, of course, like the fact that it encourages those corny "none of your beeswax!" jokes  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>It might interest you to know that one of the nuts came off my exhaust manifold flange.  It was just tack welded on, and somehow it came off.  Chances are, I'll just replace it with a separate nut.  And I plan to use anti-seize on everything when I put it all back together.     ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Wannabe||01-28-2001||10:20 PM||||Heat. Torch the crap out of them. Even if you pick out one of those toy oxy/propane brazing/light welding kits from the hardware store, the hassle it will save in removing rusty old bolts is well worth the $40-50 price of these kits.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mattsfiero||01-29-2001||08:23 AM||||Hey worst case cenario if the bolt does snap before attempting to drill and retap hit it with the all purpose Easy out wich may help you get it with a ratchet instead of Vice Grips........Worst case other than that I hear the Torching it  very well it works like a charm.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||10
Z||000008||bHooper||01-29-2001||08:48 AM||||oh, believe me... i am fully aware of the effectiveness of a blow torch!<P>a buddy is going to loan me his Rockwell drill tonight, so that i can get the head fo the bolt drilled out.  i think with the crossover out of the way, i can get to the manifold flange with the torch.<P>i have already removed the slave cylinder and shifter bracket, to make room.<P>man this is turning into a job!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000009||Wade87GT||01-29-2001||09:17 AM||||I suggest that if you are going to drill out a broken bolt, just keep going and retap the threads on the flange when you're done. I'm working on the same problem at the "out" end of the crossover pipe. I had soaked the bolts with penetrating oil for more than a day previously. One bolt head sheared off effortlessly and the other came out as intended. I tried using an easy-out, which broke off in the bolt! It may sound very amateurish, but for now I'm using a C-clamp on the one side until the engine comes out for my 3.4 project. ||||reg||9
Z||000010||Raydar||01-29-2001||11:30 AM||||Three of my crossover bolts came out after marinating in Liquid Wrench overnight. The fourth one (firewall side, bottom) was too rusted away for the socket to grip. I ended up cutting it off with a zizz wheel (is that a southern expression?), removing the crossover, then turning out the rest of the bolt with vice grips. Was not a lot of fun, but it worked.<BR>If yours has enough thread sticking out on the back side of the nut, you might be able to grab it there.<BR>I replaced all four bolts with stainless.<BR>I've also heard that the nuts are only spot welded to the manifold flanges. You might be able to break it off and just use a nut and bolt. <BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||01-29-2001||11:55 AM||||hmmmmm.... C clamps :curious:<P>holding the flanges compressed?  what a great idea!  does it put enough pressure on it to prevent any exhaust leaks?<P>i might pick up a pair of these just in case i dont get very far, tonight.<P>it's been 3 days without my car, and i am already missing her!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wade87GT||01-29-2001||02:52 PM||||Please don't laugh, but I really needed the car and I actually had one side of the flange held together with a pair of vice-grips. Bolted the other side. Drove like that for about a week till I came up with using a 1.2 inch c-clamp. After you've got it tight just tie down that sliding rod with some safety wire. I've had it up to 5500 RPM numerous times without any leaks so far, even with the vice-grips||||reg||11
Z||000013||bHooper||01-29-2001||04:36 PM||||do you see me laughing?  i'm not laughing.  heck, that'll probably wind up as my only solution, minus the vice grips of course.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000014||bHooper||01-29-2001||08:36 PM||||well, i finally got the head of the bolt ground off... it took 3 grinding points to do it!<P>however, now i have another problem... the bolt will turn in the nut, but the back side that was exposed is severely coroded and won't go through the nut!  and the short broken one doesnt turn at all.<P>looks like i'm in for more grinding!<P>hoop||||reg||1
